WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:03.760
We are gathered here today to
celebrate Ruby and Sapphire.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.345
Narrator: You're looking
at the first LGBTQ wedding

00:00:06.345 --> 00:00:08.237
in a kids' animated show.

00:00:08.237 --> 00:00:10.740
And this is the first
kids' animated character

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.970
to directly identify themselves as gay.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.070
Benson: Yes!

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.750
Because I'm gay.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.240
Narrator: These are just a few examples

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.920
in a recent push to include
more queer representation

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.230
in children's animated shows and movies.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.100
But it took a long time and
a lot of work to get here,

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.480
battling decades of harmful stereotypes

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.350
and queer-coded villains.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:31.930
Rebecca Sugar: You know,
it was just a matter of

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:33.800
not so much busting down these walls,

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.660
but just very slowly chipping away at them

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.817
for years and years and years and years.

00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:41.290
Narrator: Let's take a
look at how we got to this

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:42.385
from this.

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.360
This is a scene from the 1933 animated

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.880
Flip the Frog short "Soda Squirt."

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:52.840
The flamboyant character
turned Hyde-like monster

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.390
pretty much sums up to the extent

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.050
to which "queer" characters
appeared on screen at the time.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.080
A year later, in 1934,

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.420
the Motion Picture Association of America

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.250
started enforcing the Hays Code,

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.295
which banned in films, quote,

00:01:05.295 --> 00:01:07.930
"Sex perversion or any inference to it."

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.610
That was the same year the
US government established

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.500
the Federal Communications Commission

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.650
and the first set of
commercial TV regulations.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.020
This created an era of internal censorship

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.510
out of fear of being hit
with reprimands, fines,

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.180
or having their broadcast license revoked.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.710
But creators did get creative,

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.050
especially when it came to animated films.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.820
Melanie Kohnen: People actually found ways

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.640
of inserting queerness into cartoons

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.530
and stretched the boundaries
of the Production Code,

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.330
because animation in itself is a medium

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:39.660
that already lends itself

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.290
to surrealness or strange situations.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.950
Narrator: Live-action TV
couldn't show two men kissing,

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.940
but a character like Bugs
Bunny could kiss hunter Elmer

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.548
and pass it off as a joke.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.210
Speech patterns, mannerisms,
costumes, and more

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.550
also became signals of
queerness, or queer coding.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.400
Let's take a look at Bugs Bunny again,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.440
who first appeared in 1940.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.990
In addition to kissing other
male characters on the mouth,

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.752
he also cross-dresses in many episodes.

00:02:06.752 --> 00:02:08.730
But this didn't make him queer.

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.410
According to creator Chuck Jones,

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.400
this was all meant to make him "funny."

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.250
Viewers, however, could have
their own interpretation.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.765
RuPaul, for one, has said that

00:02:16.765 --> 00:02:19.490
Bugs Bunny was his "first
introduction to drag."

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.670
Cartoon characters were
also given the freedom

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.820
to veer from traditional gender roles,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:24.850
so we could see characters

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.230
like an effeminate bull
who refused to fight

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.160
or a tea-sipping dragon.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.740
Queer coding may also have
been a way around censorship,

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.020
but it wasn't ideal and
relied on harmful stereotypes.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.040
And as television grew in popularity,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.400
these stereotypes would
play out again and again,

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.393
sometimes in damaging ways.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:43.213
The '50s.

00:02:43.213 --> 00:02:45.480
A time known for conformity in the States

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.580
and for the television cartoon boom.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.000
Both those things came into play

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.840
with the Code of Practices
for Television Broadcasters,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.160
a set of evolving guidelines

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.880
that covered everything
from decency and decorum

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.580
to TV's responsibility towards children.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.210
The original 1952 code stated that

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.420
"Sex crimes and abnormalities"
were unacceptable topics

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.630
and that shows should foster
"commonly accepted moral,

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:11.250
social and ethical ideals
characteristic of American life."

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:12.130
This was all happening

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.340
with McCarthy's Lavender
Scare in the backdrop,

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.130
and it became visible on screen.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.970
Queer characters were no longer
just a source of ridicule,

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:20.332
but violently attacked,

00:03:20.332 --> 00:03:22.750
fueling a decades-long
"bury your gays" trope

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.010
that persists today.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.170
Many popular characters were still coded

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.660
from the '60s to the '80s,

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:29.330
despite a sexual revolution

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.240
and the start of the gay-rights movement.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:32.830
But queer-coded characters

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:35.340
were also increasingly cast as villains.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.681
Melanie: If you go with the classic

00:03:36.681 --> 00:03:38.306
hero-versus-villain model,

00:03:38.306 --> 00:03:40.967
the hero will embody what's normal,

00:03:40.967 --> 00:03:42.800
and then the villain
has to be the opposite.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.120
And an easy way to suggest that is

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.470
to draw on really obvious stereotypes

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:50.560
that suggest queerness.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.950
Narrator: Let's look at the earliest entry

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:53.818
in Insider's database that cataloged

00:03:53.818 --> 00:03:56.830
more than 200 confirmed LGBT characters.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:00.282
This is Skeleton, a villain
in the 1980s show "SuperTed."

00:04:00.282 --> 00:04:04.168
The creator confirmed Skeleton's
sexuality as gay in 2014.

00:04:04.168 --> 00:04:06.530
Skeleton: Do you think I look
best in diamonds or rubies?

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:09.140
Narrator: Many iconic
queer-coded Disney movie villains

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.300
emerged in the late '80s and '90s.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.962
Ursula from "The Little Mermaid"

00:04:12.962 --> 00:04:14.970
was inspired by the drag queen Divine,

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.670
and queer communities
welcomed her with open arms.

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:19.030
Melanie: Audiences may, in fact,

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:20.892
embrace the villain over the hero

00:04:20.892 --> 00:04:25.285
because the villain offers
an alternative vision of life

00:04:25.285 --> 00:04:27.590
or an alternative life path,

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:30.780
and one that doesn't end
in heterosexual romance.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.813
Narrator: Other characters
like Gaston and LeFou

00:04:32.813 --> 00:04:33.870
from "Beauty and the Beast"

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.300
and Jafar from "Aladdin"

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:37.210
could have been influenced
by their makers.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:40.850
They were created by Andreas
Deja, an openly gay animator,

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:44.755
and sang music written by openly
gay lyricist Howard Ashman.

00:04:44.755 --> 00:04:48.090
But that sliver of
representation had its downfalls.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.510
If you can only exist as a
villain or a joke, I mean,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:54.620
that's a really heavy thing
to be saturated with as a kid.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.280
Narrator: And despite code improvements,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.590
networks were still hesitant

00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:59.998
to show any obvious representation.

00:04:59.998 --> 00:05:01.680
Powerpuff narrator: The
only safe way to refer

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.037
to this king of darkness is simply ...

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.030
Him.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.390
Narrator: Animators described
a "culture of fear,"

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:10.810
with studios worried
about losing advertisers

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.440
and boycotts from conservative
groups and angry parents.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.390
This fear was realized in 2005

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:18.820
with an episode of
"Postcards from Buster,"

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.498
an "Arthur" spin-off series.

00:05:20.498 --> 00:05:22.570
"Sugartime" featured two lesbian moms,

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.860
one of the earliest overt
queer representations

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.130
in children's animation.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.230
In response, Margaret Spellings,

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.000
the then secretary of education,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.370
said PBS "should strongly consider"

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.870
returning the funds it
used for the episode.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:38.300
It was a major setback for queer
representation at the time.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.258
14 years later, an episode of "Arthur"

00:05:40.258 --> 00:05:42.300
featured the anthropomorphic aardvark

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:43.942
attending the wedding of his teacher,

00:05:43.942 --> 00:05:46.340
Mr. Ratburn, to his partner, Patrick,

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:47.440
and strangely enough,

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.950
we have the rise of cable
TV and eventually streaming

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:51.400
to thank for that.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:54.060
With fewer regulations and
less government oversight,

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.990
cable TV opened the door
for more representation.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:59.150
But getting anything approved
for children to watch,

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.940
whether it was commercials
for bike helmets or junk food,

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:03.550
remained a long process,

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:04.640
with requests having to go

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.030
through seven or eight levels of approval.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:09.020
This could add extra hurdles for creators,

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.990
and did for Rebecca Sugar during her time

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:12.230
at Cartoon Network.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.880
In 2010, Rebecca was
working on "Adventure Time"

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.720
and floated the idea of a
past lesbian relationship

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.100
between Princess Bubblegum
and the vampire Marceline.

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.240
But even then --

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.270
It was pretty much impossible

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.940
to have characters in an animated show

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:29.940
that were openly LGBTQIA.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:31.890
Narrator: Pendleton
Ward, the show's creator,

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.150
supported this idea.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.353
But Rebecca said she
faced years of pushback

00:06:35.353 --> 00:06:37.397
from the studio, which was concerned about

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:40.373
distribution in countries
where being gay or lesbian

00:06:40.373 --> 00:06:42.970
was censored in media
and considered a crime.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:45.010
Rebecca: When we tried
to talk about it openly,

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.610
the hammer came down,
and they basically --

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.170
it went all the way to the
top and came down hard on us.

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:52.280
Narrator: The characters'
relationship wasn't confirmed

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:56.030
until 2018 when the two
kissed in the season finale.

00:06:56.030 --> 00:06:58.020
After her experience
with "Adventure Time,"

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.000
Rebecca decided to create her own show

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.250
and push the boundaries even further.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:03.521
♪ We ♪

00:07:03.521 --> 00:07:04.787
♪ Are the Crystal ♪

00:07:04.787 --> 00:07:05.820
♪ Gems ♪

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:06.833
Narrator: So in 2012,

00:07:06.833 --> 00:07:08.910
she started developing "Steven Universe,"

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:10.900
about a gender-nonconforming boy

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:13.900
who is friends with
extraterrestrial nonbinary beings,

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:14.820
or gems.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.278
In season one, viewers learn

00:07:16.278 --> 00:07:18.290
that two of those gems are in love,

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.170
but revealing this relationship
was a slow process,

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:23.450
and each step was a
struggle with the network.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:24.620
Rebecca says she received

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.580
specific feedback from Cartoon Network

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.482
that the two characters couldn't kiss.

00:07:28.482 --> 00:07:30.214
Rebecca: And it all
became very disturbing,

00:07:30.214 --> 00:07:33.520
and realizing that I was sort
of getting these direct notes,

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:34.730
this really made me realize

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:37.050
that I had been receiving
that message indirectly

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:39.788
from all of the content
that I had seen growing up,

00:07:39.788 --> 00:07:42.710
where LGBTQIA characters
just aren't present.

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:44.850
And they aren't present
because they're being,

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:46.890
you know, blocked from existing.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.740
You know, they're being
systematically removed.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:50.410
Narrator: Their relationship
was first hinted at

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:52.220
in an episode called "Jail Break,"

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:53.920
which aired in March 2015,

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:55.480
just as same-sex marriage was about

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:56.980
to become legal in the US.

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:57.996
Rebecca: Even the audience members

00:07:57.996 --> 00:08:00.504
who were excited about it or who liked it

00:08:00.504 --> 00:08:02.120
would say things like,

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:03.442
"I can't believe you're getting

00:08:03.442 --> 00:08:05.183
these adult themes into your show."

00:08:05.183 --> 00:08:08.120
It was all treated as if
it was very subversive.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:12.390
It's not that we snuck adult
ideas into a children's show.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.830
I mean, children are flooded

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:16.900
with heteronormative content all the time.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:18.410
Narrator: Most animated Disney films,

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:19.502
like "Sleeping Beauty,"

00:08:19.502 --> 00:08:21.890
involve the love between
a princess and a prince.

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:23.210
Those scenes inspired Rebecca

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:25.250
to create ones for queer audiences.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:26.940
You know, I wanted to experiment

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.970
with some of these really classic visuals.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.761
Narrator: In 2015, Rebecca says,

00:08:31.761 --> 00:08:32.970
studio executives told her

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:36.280
it was her choice to make
Ruby and Sapphire LGBTQ.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:38.950
They warned her that some
countries might pull the show

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.498
and it could get canceled altogether.

00:08:41.498 --> 00:08:42.373
But the next year,

00:08:42.373 --> 00:08:45.250
Rebecca came out as bisexual
at San Diego Comic-Con

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:48.250
and began to speak more
publicly about LGBTQ issues,

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:51.110
both in the industry and in her work.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:52.870
It's really hard to twist yourself

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:54.650
into a different person to make art.

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:57.510
It was hard for me to try
and make heteronormative art,

00:08:57.510 --> 00:08:59.714
and that's part of the
reason I ultimately realized

00:08:59.714 --> 00:09:02.323
that I could come out and
I could speak about this

00:09:02.323 --> 00:09:04.140
and that it was a part of who I am.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:06.130
Narrator: Coming out helped
her speak more openly

00:09:06.130 --> 00:09:09.120
and advocate for her
characters to be LGBTQ,

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.710
and it paid off.

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.480
In 2017, "Steven Universe" was nominated

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:15.830
for the GLAAD outstanding
comedy series award,

00:09:15.830 --> 00:09:19.410
and in 2019, it won in the
kids and family category.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:21.760
And then, all of a sudden,

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.770
the force of decades and decades

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:27.710
of activism and advocacy
were suddenly behind me.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.860
Narrator: The first gay
proposal and wedding episode

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:31.680
aired in the summer of 2018

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.970
and made kids' animation history.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.605
Steven: I now pronounce you Garnet!

00:09:38.517 --> 00:09:40.413
Narrator: "Steven Universe"
was the start of a wave

00:09:40.413 --> 00:09:43.140
of animated shows with
LGBTQ representation,

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.690
and the rise of streaming apps

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:47.060
would make that wave even bigger.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:49.850
Without FCC regulations or broadcast codes

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:51.530
and with fewer layers of approval,

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:54.115
creators were given more
autonomy with their characters.

00:09:54.115 --> 00:09:55.420
This is where we start seeing

00:09:55.420 --> 00:09:57.520
much more representation in animation,

00:09:57.520 --> 00:09:59.540
and not only with sexual orientation,

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:02.680
but with gender identity and
queer communities overall.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:06.020
Velma from the original
1969 "Scooby-Doo" cartoon

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:07.240
was long read as gay

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:10.670
before being officially written
as lesbian in a 2010 update,

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:12.180
"Mystery Incorporated."

00:10:12.180 --> 00:10:13.630
Benson, a leading character

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:15.460
from "Kipo and the Age of Wonderbeasts,"

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:18.280
made history as the first
kids' animated character

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:20.880
to directly identify as gay in dialogue.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:21.980
Benson: Yes!

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:24.590
Because I'm gay.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:25.652
Narrator: And Noelle Stevenson's

00:10:25.652 --> 00:10:27.540
"She-Ra and the Princesses of Power"

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.010
featured a multiseason queer story arc,

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:33.441
capped off in 2020 by
a famous on-screen kiss

00:10:33.441 --> 00:10:35.050
between Adora and Catra.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:36.350
The show also broke ground

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.263
by featuring a trans male
character, Jewelstar,

00:10:39.263 --> 00:10:42.350
voiced by trans male
actor Alex Blue Davis.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:44.187
From 2017 to 2019,

00:10:44.187 --> 00:10:47.220
our database showed a more than 200% spike

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.410
in queer and gender-minority characters

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.580
in children's animated TV shows.

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:53.430
We need to have queer representation

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:56.850
in children's animation
between queer children exist

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:59.390
and they deserve to see
themselves on the screen.

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:02.720
They deserve to see romance and happiness

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.660
that represents the things
that they already feel.

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:06.990
Narrator: There are still some limitations

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:08.380
to the progress, though.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.570
90% of the characters are
behind a paywall of some sort,

00:11:11.570 --> 00:11:13.610
preventing all children from equal access

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:15.360
to LGBT representation.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:17.960
And the stories don't always
do the characters justice.

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:19.750
Shiro, the leader of the Paladins

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:21.460
in "Voltron: Legendary Defender,"

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.780
was revealed to be gay at
the 2018 San Diego Comic-Con,

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:26.210
but his partner was killed off

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:28.370
in the same episode he was introduced in,

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:31.150
a controversial moment that
played into negative tropes

00:11:31.150 --> 00:11:33.160
and didn't sit well with fans.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:34.450
But just like Rebecca Sugar

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:36.010
pushed the boundaries in the past,

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:37.600
a new generation of animators

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:39.360
is raising the bar for the future.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:40.870
What I hope for in the future

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:42.870
is that more and more marginalized artists

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:44.110
are going to be in a position

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:45.560
to make the art that they want to make

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:49.535
and to reach people with that
art who can relate to it.

